  "claim_boundary": "This frozen D0 pre-death-test retains all seven Structure-Two capabilities and defines legal compute paths, counterfactual branching, reversible inference debt, hard safety constraints, best-fixed selection, and hindsight headroom testing. Architecture A, the transitively read-only local P0-P5 plan and consumption registries, partial path kernels, a runtime-owned local authorization policy, different-location detected full CIAV closure, same-location detected fast verification, an isolated debt-replay entrypoint, caller-context snapshots on both adaptive public entrypoints, a pending-debt core-transition barrier, and measured executed-operator elapsed receipts are source-bound. The barrier does not freeze every wrapper rebinding, replay does not bind origin policy/trace, and a negative CIAV observation does not yet enter a complete downstream closure. The production runtime does not yet derive the router features from production state; P1, P2, and P4 do not yet implement distinct consequential compute kernels; the registered timeout/failure fallback state machine is not implemented; and feature-extraction plus router overhead are not measured. A typed sink acknowledgement and compensating abort protocol do not establish durable cross-system trace/state atomicity. The legacy no-plan/no-trace call shape is present, but this gate alone is not a behavioral-equivalence proof. The protocol also does not bind path-specific operator budgets, a frozen exhaustive branch manifest, eager-reference recovery equivalence, empirical power, independent confirmatory custody, exogenous production RNG, or long-horizon action consequences; train or validate a deployable router; establish an observable-information ceiling; provide external validity; authorize seven-operator ablation; or support a scientific superiority or novelty claim."
